 ## Overview
-Human and Avatar Modeling extends static scene representation to handle dynamic, non-rigid subjects, specifically humans, by integrating parametric body models or deformation priors with neural radiance fields [G]. Unlike static methods that assume rigid geometry, this family solves the problem of rendering articulated bodies with moving poses and expressions from limited input views (monocular or sparse multi-view). It is distinguished from sibling methods like [Specific Model Architectures](8585-specific-model-architectures.md) by its explicit coupling of neural fields with anatomical constraints (e.g., SMPL skeletons or blendshapes) to resolve the ambiguity of non-rigid deformation [G].
