 ### ARAH Pipeline
-A model to create animatable clothed human avatars with detailed geometry that generalizes to out-of-distribution poses, combining an articulated implicit surface representation (SDF) with volume rendering [S2]. It uses a novel joint root-finding algorithm to simultaneously search for the intersection with the SDF iso-surface in observation space and the correspondence in canonical space [S2]. The model consists of three canonical-space networks: a forward linear blend skinning (LBS) network, a canonical SDF network, and a canonical color network, all trained end-to-end with photometric and regularization losses [S2].
+A model to create animatable clothed human avatars with detailed geometry that generalizes to out-of-distribution poses, combining an articulated implicit surface representation (SDF) with volume rendering [S2]. It uses a novel joint root-finding algorithm to simultaneously search for the intersection with the SDF iso-surface in observation space and the correspondence in canonical space [S2]. The model consists of three canonical-space networks: a forward linear blend skinning (LBS) network $LBS_{\boldsymbol{\theta}, \boldsymbol{\eta}}$, a canonical SDF network $f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_f}$, and a canonical color network $f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_c}$, all trained end-to-end with photometric and regularization losses [S2]. These networks do not condition on inputs in observation space [S2].
 
 ## Background
-The field emerged from the need to render dynamic humans, which standard NeRF cannot handle due to its rigid scene assumption [G]. Early works like [NeuMan](12480-neuman.md) and [Neural Body](12176-neural-body.md) introduced parametric models (SMPL) to guide deformation. Facial modeling was addressed by [NeRF with Per-Frame Latent Codes](12042-nerf-with-per-frame-latent-codes.md) using blendshapes and latent codes. More recent approaches like Xu et al. and ARAH focus on relighting and generalization to unseen poses using SDFs and advanced deformation fields [S1, S2].
